  "successCriteria": "Nakasendo / Kiso Valley is locked as the hiking anchor with a concrete two-day route (Magome–Tsumago, Tsumago–Nagiso) plus an optional Narai/Yabuhara extension day, day-by-day distances and post-town lodging, and rail + luggage-forwarding logistics that connect Tokyo and Kyoto without a major detour.",
  "rationale": "Anchoring on the Nakasendo keeps the hiking on the existing Tokyo–Kyoto corridor, so it adds scenery and a cultural high point without forcing a separate side-trip — unlike alternatives such as the Kumano Kodo (far south, needs its own loop) or Kamikochi/Japan Alps (a northern detour off the main line). Structuring the plan as 'lock the segment → define days/distances → fit transport → book lodging' front-loads the decision that everything else depends on, so later booking and logistics steps don't have to be redone. With the two-day shape now settled, the remaining work is execution detail rather than open scoping.",
